Building the SLUDGE engine
**************************

   We build the engine with CMake 3.24 or later, on Linux, macOS and
Windows:

     cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
     cmake --build build

   The engine uses SDL 3, libogg, libvorbis, libpng, DUMB and, for movies,
libvpx and the WebM parser of libwebm. By default, CMake uses the versions
installed on the system and builds from source those that it does not
find; the WebM parser, a single source file, it always builds from source.
On Debian and Ubuntu, the packages are libvorbis-dev, libpng-dev,
libdumb1-dev and libvpx-dev, and libsdl3-dev where it exists.

   You can change the build with these options.

     -DSLUDGE_BUNDLED_DEPS=ON   Build every library from source and link it
                                statically, for an executable that runs on
                                its own.
     -DSLUDGE_MOVIES=OFF        Leave out movie support, libvpx and libwebm.
     -DSLUDGE_GLES2=ON          Draw with OpenGL ES 2.0 instead of OpenGL.
     -DSLUDGE_RENDERER_GPU=OFF  Leave out the SDL_GPU renderer (see
                                Renderers below), which is already left
                                out with OpenGL ES 2.0.
     -DSLUDGE_DEVICE=<device>   Build for the OpenPandora, the Raspberry Pi
                                or an ODROID, with OpenPandora, RaspberryPi
                                or ODROID. We then draw with OpenGL ES 2.0,
                                and on the OpenPandora, we always show the
                                game in full screen.
     -DSLUDGE_TESTS=OFF         Leave out the tests. We build them by
                                default, and you run them with
                                `ctest --test-dir build'.
     -DSLUDGE_DEVKIT=OFF        Leave out the Dev Kit, which we build by
                                default on Linux (see below).
     -DSLUDGE_ENGINE=OFF        Leave out the engine, for example to build
                                only the Dev Kit.
     -DSLUDGE_COMPILER=OFF      Leave out the headless compiler,
                                sludge-compiler (see below). The Dev Kit
                                needs it.
     -DSLUDGE_DOC=ON            Install the documentation and the example
                                projects.

   On macOS, build each architecture separately, for example with
-DCMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES=x86_64, and combine the executables with lipo.
Our releases run on macOS 10.13 and later: we build the Intel half with
-DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=10.13 and the Apple Silicon half with 11.0.
Before macOS 10.14, SDL_GPU can't draw with Metal, and the engine draws
with OpenGL.
With packaging/macos/make-app.sh, we make an application bundle around
the engine.

   On Windows, we build with MSYS2 and MinGW. For 32-bit Windows, from
Windows XP on, we cross-compile on Linux with
-DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/toolchains/mingw-i686.cmake and
-DSLUDGE_BUNDLED_DEPS=ON. For that, the MinGW-w64 compilers of the win32
thread model and nasm are required (g++-mingw-w64-i686-win32 and nasm on
Debian and Ubuntu).

   Run the engine with the game data file as its argument, for example
`build/sludge-engine gamedata.slg'. Run `build/sludge-engine --help' for
the options.

Renderers
=========

   The engine draws through the interface in source/Engine/renderer.h,
with OpenGL (renderer_gl.cpp) or with SDL_GPU (renderer_gpu.cpp), which
draws with Metal on macOS, Direct3D 12 on Windows and Vulkan on Linux. By
default (auto), the engine draws with SDL_GPU. When one renderer can't
draw, the engine uses the other, for example OpenGL on Windows without
Direct3D 12, on Linux without a Vulkan driver, or on macOS before 10.14.
It prints which renderer it uses, for example `Renderer: SDL_GPU (metal)'.

   To choose the renderer, set RENDERER to auto, gpu or gl in the
settings file of the game: on macOS,
~/Library/Application Support/SLUDGE/<game>/<data file>.ini, and on Linux,
~/.sludge-engine/<game>/<data file>.ini. The option --renderer and the
environment variable SLUDGE_RENDERER choose for one run, in that order
before the settings file. --gpu-driver, or SDL's environment variable
SDL_GPU_DRIVER, chooses the driver of SDL_GPU: metal, vulkan or
direct3d12.

   The shaders of SDL_GPU are in source/Engine/shaders/gpu, in GLSL 4.50.
We compile them to SPIR-V for Vulkan, translate that to MSL for Metal and
to HLSL, which we compile to DXIL for Direct3D 12, and keep the result in
gpu_shaders.h, so that building the engine needs no shader tools. After
changing a shader, run

     cmake -P cmake/GenerateGPUShaders.cmake

which needs glslangValidator (glslang) and spirv-cross (both in Homebrew
and in most Linux distributions), and dxc, the DirectX Shader Compiler,
1.8 or later, so that it signs the DXIL, which Direct3D 12 wants. dxc
comes with the Vulkan SDK (https://vulkan.lunarg.com), and for Linux and
Windows also from
https://github.com/microsoft/DirectXShaderCompiler/releases.

Capture tests
=============

   The capture tests play games deterministically and compare their
frames, so that we notice when a change to the engine changes what it
draws. With --capture=<folder>, the engine runs a game without sound, with
a clock that advances by one frame per frame and with the mouse and
keyboard events of an input script (--input). It writes a CRC of every
frame to frames.tsv, and the frames chosen with --frames as PNG files.
source/Engine/capture.h describes the options and the input scripts, and
--record-input writes an input script while you play.

   The tests play the example projects, tests/render/RenderTest.slp, which
has a scene for each way the engine draws and ends with a movie, and Out Of
Order when you give its data file with -DSLUDGE_OUT_OF_ORDER=<file>
(packaging/get-out-of-order.sh downloads it). Each test plays its game
twice, and the frames must be the same; with OpenGL, the frames that we
keep as images may differ by 1 in a colour channel, which some virtual
GPUs do. With -DSLUDGE_CAPTURE_REFERENCE_ENGINE=<engine>, each test also
compares the frames with those of another build of the engine, for
example of an earlier commit. With -DSLUDGE_GPU_CAPTURE_TESTS=ON, the
default on macOS and Windows (on Linux, Mesa's software Vulkan, lavapipe,
is enough), each test also plays its game twice with SDL_GPU, and
compares the frames of SDL_GPU with those of OpenGL; there, up to 5% of
the pixels of a frame may differ, where the two round differently. On
Windows, set SDL_GPU_DRIVER=direct3d12 to test Direct3D 12; without an
OpenGL 2.0 driver, copy Mesa's opengl32.dll (mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-mesa
in MSYS2) next to the engine and set GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe. The tests
need a display and have the label capture:

     ctest --test-dir build -L capture     Only the capture tests
     ctest --test-dir build -LE capture    All the other tests

   tests/framediff compares two capture folders or two PNG files, and
writes the images that differ to build/capture/<test>/difference.

Making the release packages
***************************

   In .github/workflows/build.yml, we build and test the engine on Linux,
macOS and Windows. For a version tag, such as 2.3.0, we publish a release
with two kinds of packages for macOS and Windows: OpenSLUDGE, the engine
alone, and Out Of Order, the engine with the game Out Of Order, and with
the source tarball, sludge-<version>.tar.gz. We make the packages with the
scripts in the packaging folder.

     get-out-of-order.sh        Download the data file of Out Of Order from
                                the Ubuntu archive and check its hash.
     windows/make-zips.sh       Make the zips of OpenSLUDGE and of Out Of
                                Order for Windows.
     macos/make-app.sh          Make a macOS application bundle, with or
                                without a game.
     macos/sign-app.sh          Sign a macOS application bundle.
     make-tarball.sh            Make the source tarball with git archive,
                                without the files marked export-ignore in
                                .gitattributes.

   Out Of Order is freeware, and anyone may give it away together with its
readme and licence. We change nothing in the game, and only add a
controller mapping for it, packaging/out-of-order/controller.ini. To
publish only OpenSLUDGE, leave out the Out Of Order zips and apps in the
workflow. In the tests of the workflow, we play the opening of Out Of
Order, so keep get-out-of-order.sh for those.

Building the SLUDGE compiler
****************************

   The compiler, sludge-compiler, turns a SLUDGE project (.slp) into a game
data file (.slg). It runs without a display and needs only libpng, which
CMake builds from source if it is missing, so it builds on Linux, macOS
and Windows without GTK. To build only the compiler, run

     cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DSLUDGE_ENGINE=OFF \
       -DSLUDGE_DEVKIT=OFF
     cmake --build build

and compile a project with `build/source/DevKit/sludge-compiler
<project.slp>'. It writes the game file next to the project file, and
`sludge-compiler --help' lists its options. On Windows, it builds with
MinGW, as the engine does. The 32-bit Windows build in the workflow,
which we cross-compile on Linux, builds only the engine
(-DSLUDGE_COMPILER=OFF).

   With the function sludge_compile_game in cmake/SludgeCompileGame.cmake,
we compile projects when we build: the example projects in
doc/ExampleProjects go to build/games/Welcome.slg and
build/games/VerbCoin.slg. `ctest --test-dir build' checks their hashes
against tests/games.sha256, so that we notice when the compiler makes
different game files. When the compiler changes on purpose, update the
hashes with

     (cd build/games && shasum -a 256 Welcome.slg VerbCoin.slg) \
       > tests/games.sha256

Building the SLUDGE Development Kit
***********************************

Windows and Mac OS X
====================

   So far, we build the Dev Kit with GTK 3 only on Linux. On macOS, the
compiler builds without the Dev Kit (see above). The Windows
installer script, sludge.nsi, is for the earlier Dev Kit with GTK+ 2.16
and GtkGLExt, and the Xcode project in the "Mac Dev Kit" directory is
for Mac OS X 10.4, so we cannot build the current Dev Kit with either of
them.

Linux
=====

   Dependencies of the OpenSLUDGE Dev Kit are GTK 3 (>= 3.20), libepoxy
and libpng. We draw the views of the floor maker, the z-buffer maker and
the sprite bank editor with OpenGL 3.2.

   On Debian and Ubuntu the corresponding packages are libgtk-3-dev,
libepoxy-dev and libpng-dev.

   On Linux, CMake builds the Dev Kit together with the engine, with the
commands at the top of this file. To build and install only the Dev Kit,
run

     cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DSLUDGE_ENGINE=OFF
     cmake --build build
     sudo cmake --install build

   Add -DSLUDGE_DOC=ON to install the documentation too. For the
documentation only, use -DSLUDGE_ENGINE=OFF -DSLUDGE_DEVKIT=OFF
-DSLUDGE_DOC=ON.

   With `ctest --test-dir build', we also draw the views of the floor
maker, the z-buffer maker and the sprite bank editor offscreen and compare
them with the reference images in tests/devkit/references. For that,
Mesa's EGL (libegl-mesa0 on Debian and Ubuntu) is required.
